# Boolean laws — the "Binary" list of aPToP §11.3.1 (Hehner, A Practical
# Theory of Programming), as a Netty law list.
#
# A line is a comment when it is blank or begins with '#'. Otherwise it is a
# law: an optional name before the first ':', then a boolean expression. The
# identifiers of the expression are universally quantified, so the line
#
#     specialization: a ∧ b ⇒ a
#
# is the law ∀a, b: bool· a ∧ b ⇒ a. Write '∀a, b· …' if you want to quantify
# some other set of identifiers, and '∀· …' to quantify none.
#
# '≡ ⟹ ⟸' are the book's large '= ⇒ ⇐': the same operators at the lowest
# precedence, which a law file needs because it has no left margin to supply
# them. Remember that aPToP's '¬' binds more weakly than '=', so 'a = ¬b' does
# not parse; write 'a ≡ ¬b' or 'a = (¬b)'.
#
# 'if a then b else c fi' is the document's conditional expression. It brackets
# itself, so it needs no parentheses and takes a whole expression in each of its
# three places. The words 'if', 'then', 'else' and 'fi' are reserved.

mirror: a ⇐ b ≡ b ⇒ a
double negation: ¬¬a ≡ a

excluded middle: a ∨ ¬a
noncontradiction: ¬(a ∧ ¬a)

base: ¬(a ∧ ⊥)
base: a ∨ ⊤
base: a ⇒ ⊤
base: ⊥ ⇒ a

identity: ⊤ ∧ a ≡ a
identity: ⊥ ∨ a ≡ a
identity: ⊤ ⇒ a ≡ a
identity: ⊤ = a ≡ a

idempotent: a ∧ a ≡ a
idempotent: a ∨ a ≡ a
reflexive: a ⇒ a
reflexive: a = a

indirect proof: ¬a ⇒ ⊥ ≡ a
indirect proof: ¬a ⇒ a ≡ a
specialization: a ∧ b ⇒ a
generalization: a ⇒ a ∨ b

associative: a ∧ (b ∧ c) ≡ (a ∧ b) ∧ c
associative: a ∨ (b ∨ c) ≡ (a ∨ b) ∨ c
associative: (a = (b = c)) ≡ ((a = b) = c)
associative: (a ⧧ (b ⧧ c)) ≡ ((a ⧧ b) ⧧ c)

symmetry: a ∧ b ≡ b ∧ a
symmetry: a ∨ b ≡ b ∨ a
symmetry: (a = b) ≡ (b = a)
symmetry: (a ⧧ b) ≡ (b ⧧ a)

antisymmetry: (a ⇒ b) ∧ (b ⇒ a) ≡ (a = b)
discharge: a ∧ (a ⇒ b) ≡ a ∧ b
discharge: a ⇒ a ∧ b ≡ a ⇒ b

antimonotonic: a ⇒ b ≡ ¬a ⇐ ¬b
monotonic: (a ⇒ b) ⇒ (a ∧ c ⇒ b ∧ c)
monotonic: (a ⇒ b) ⇒ (a ∨ c ⇒ b ∨ c)
monotonic: (a ⇒ b) ⇒ ((c ⇒ a) ⇒ (c ⇒ b))
antimonotonic: (a ⇒ b) ⇒ ((a ⇒ c) ⇐ (b ⇒ c))

duality: ¬(a ∧ b) ≡ ¬a ∨ ¬b
duality: ¬(a ∨ b) ≡ ¬a ∧ ¬b

exclusion: (a ⇒ ¬b) ≡ (b ⇒ ¬a)
exclusion: (a ≡ ¬b) ≡ (a ⧧ b)
exclusion: (a ⧧ b) ≡ (¬a ≡ b)

inclusion: a ⇒ b ≡ ¬a ∨ b
inclusion: a ⇒ b ≡ ((a ∧ b) = a)
inclusion: a ⇒ b ≡ ((a ∨ b) = b)

absorption: a ∧ (a ∨ b) ≡ a
absorption: a ∨ (a ∧ b) ≡ a

direct proof: (a ⇒ b) ∧ a ⇒ b
direct proof: (a ⇒ b) ∧ ¬b ⇒ ¬a
direct proof: (a ∨ b) ∧ ¬a ⇒ b

transitive: (a ∧ b) ∧ (b ∧ c) ⇒ a ∧ c
transitive: (a ⇒ b) ∧ (b ⇒ c) ⇒ (a ⇒ c)
transitive: (a = b) ∧ (b = c) ⇒ (a = c)
transitive: (a ⇒ b) ∧ (b = c) ⇒ (a ⇒ c)
transitive: (a = b) ∧ (b ⇒ c) ⇒ (a ⇒ c)

distributive: a ∧ (b ∧ c) ≡ (a ∧ b) ∧ (a ∧ c)
distributive: a ∧ (b ∨ c) ≡ (a ∧ b) ∨ (a ∧ c)
distributive: a ∨ (b ∧ c) ≡ (a ∨ b) ∧ (a ∨ c)
distributive: a ∨ (b ⇒ c) ≡ (a ∨ b) ⇒ (a ∨ c)
distributive: (a ∨ (b = c)) ≡ ((a ∨ b) = (a ∨ c))
distributive: a ⇒ (b ∧ c) ≡ (a ⇒ b) ∧ (a ⇒ c)
distributive: a ⇒ (b ∨ c) ≡ (a ⇒ b) ∨ (a ⇒ c)
distributive: a ⇒ (b ⇒ c) ≡ (a ⇒ b) ⇒ (a ⇒ c)
distributive: (a ⇒ (b = c)) ≡ ((a ⇒ b) = (a ⇒ c))

antidistributive: a ∧ b ⇒ c ≡ (a ⇒ c) ∨ (b ⇒ c)
antidistributive: a ∨ b ⇒ c ≡ (a ⇒ c) ∧ (b ⇒ c)
portation: a ∧ b ⇒ c ≡ a ⇒ (b ⇒ c)
portation: a ∧ b ⇒ c ≡ a ⇒ ¬b ∨ c

conflation: (a ⇒ b) ∧ (c ⇒ d) ⇒ (a ∧ c ⇒ b ∧ d)
conflation: (a ⇒ b) ∧ (c ⇒ d) ⇒ (a ∨ c ⇒ b ∨ d)

equality: (a = b) ≡ (a ∧ b) ∨ (¬a ∧ ¬b)
difference: (a ⧧ b) ≡ (a ∧ ¬b) ∨ (¬a ∧ b)

# 'if … then … else … fi' (aPToP §11.3.1, the Case laws). The form is not sugar
# for anything, and Case Analysis is the law that says what it means.
case base: if T then a else b fi ≡ a
case base: if F then a else b fi ≡ b
case idempotent: if a then b else b fi ≡ b
case analysis: if a then b else c fi ≡ a ∧ b ∨ ¬a ∧ c
case reversal: if a then b else c fi ≡ if ¬a then c else b fi
